{"id":249399186658427,"type":"album","name":"Tonight’s the Night","artist":"Neil Young","year":1975,"weight":32,"info":{"cover":"https://dn710701.ca.archive.org/0/items/mbid-a75df175-7a8b-4b88-b04e-47cf44e9c17d/mbid-a75df175-7a8b-4b88-b04e-47cf44e9c17d-11436419998.jpg","kind":"Album","tracks":12,"country":"JP","yt":"RTGjVvQPVWg","links":{"rateyourmusic":"https://rateyourmusic.com/release/album/neil-young/tonights-the-night"},"desc":"Written and recorded in 1973 shortly after the death of roadie Bruce Berry, Neil Young's second close associate to die of a heroin overdose in six months (the first was Crazy Horse guitarist Danny Whitten), Tonight's the Night was Young's musical expression of grief, combined with his rejection of the stardom he had achieved in the late '60s and early '70s. The title track, performed twice, was a direct narrative about Berry: \"Bruce Berry was a working man/He used to load that Econoline van.\" Whitten was heard singing \"Come On Baby Let's Go Downtown,\" a live track recorded years earlier. ."},"artist_ref":{"id":29900611348320,"name":"Neil Young","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Neil_Young_Stavernfestivalen_2016_%28220929%29.jpg?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[{"id":185670346256268,"type":"genre","name":"rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":3782,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":109063683126539,"type":"genre","name":"Hard Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":562,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":163225726165579,"type":"genre","name":"Blues Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":458,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":90510611927654,"type":"genre","name":"Country Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":425,"yt":null,"src":"musthear,rym"},{"id":186201380116213,"type":"genre","name":"classic rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":268,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":194427380844280,"type":"genre","name":"Folk Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":621,"yt":null,"src":"rym"},{"id":13364679